KG42.register("rules-3d35b443f12a4691e49a6f77",{"id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R003","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Inter-area market clearing","process_stage":"clearing","market_meaning":"Computation and disclosure of a microgrid's purchase bid to potential sellers precede the inter-microgrid market-clearing process. Scope: Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids. Local computation of the energy quantity offered for sale precedes the distributed price-adjustment phase identified as inter-microgrid market clearing. Scope: Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids. Exchanged energy is allocated to minimize global operating cost while satisfying the microgrids' local demands. Scope: System-level allocation of energy exchanged among interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: All microgrids agree to cooperate to minimize total system cost. Each microgrid's generation plus imports must equal consumption plus exports. Local subproblems constrain offered sales, purchases and residual generation to be nonnegative. Scope: Feasible inter-microgrid allocations and the local optimization subproblems used to compute them. The aggregate energy sold by each microgrid must equal the total energy bought from it by the other microgrids; this coupling constraint governs feasible allocations rather than requiring agreement at every intermediate iteration. Scope: Feasible solutions of the reformulated inter-microgrid allocation problem (4). The allocated energy transfer E_{i,j} must be zero. Scope: Directed inter-microgrid transfer allocations. Condition: There is no directed connection from μG-i to μG-j, so a_{i,j}=0. All generation and transfer cost functions must be positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex and twice differentiable. Scope: Applicability of the proposed distributed inter-microgrid cost-minimization method. Nominal generation and link-transfer bounds may be represented by steeply rising cost functions. A microgrid may exceed its nominal generation maximum at significant additional cost. Scope: Optional representation of nominal generation and transfer-capacity bounds in the inter-microgrid allocation problem. Condition: Nominal upper bounds are introduced through cost-function soft constraints.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function clearing.inter_area under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["bidding.demand","bidding.supply","clearing.inter_area"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E001","source":"bidding.demand","target":"clearing.inter_area","type":"precedes","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e033"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E002","source":"bidding.supply","target":"clearing.inter_area","type":"precedes","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g24-e024"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Applicability of the proposed distributed inter-microgrid cost-minimization method. | Directed inter-microgrid transfer allocations. | Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids. | Feasible inter-microgrid allocations and the local optimization subproblems used to compute them. | Feasible solutions of the reformulated inter-microgrid allocation problem (4). | Optional representation of nominal generation and transfer-capacity bounds in the inter-microgrid allocation problem. | System-level allocation of energy exchanged among interconnected islanded microgrids.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e033","v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g03-e12","v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g06-e006","v141-b-g08-e008","v141-b-g11-e011","v141-b-g12-e012","v141-b-g15-e015","v141-b-g16-e016","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g24-e024"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Eij","symbol":"E_{i,j}","meaning":"Directed energy transfer allocated from microgrid i to microgrid j in the inter-microgrid clearing problem.","kind":"operational","domain":"E_{i,j} ≥ 0 for all i,j in problem (3)/(4); directed transfer quantity.","unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M","j=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g06-e006","v141-b-g15-e015"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-x-eps","symbol":"ε_i^{(s)}","meaning":"Energy sold by microgrid i in the reformulated inter-microgrid allocation problem (4), later forced equal to the energy bought from i by other microgrids.","kind":"operational","domain":"Decision variable in (4); local subproblem (6) explicitly imposes ε_i^{(s)} ≥ 0.","unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g15-e015","v141-b-g16-e016"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Eg","symbol":"E_i^{(g)}","meaning":"Energy generated by microgrid i during the scheduling interval in the system model.","kind":"operational","domain":"Energy generation quantity in MWh as used in balance (1); the supplied evidence does not state an explicit sign bound on E_i^{(g)}.","unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Eb","symbol":"E_i^{(b)}","meaning":"M-dimensional vector collecting the energy quantities bought by microgrid i from the other microgrids.","kind":"operational","domain":"Derived vector E_i^{(b)} = [E_{1,i} ... E_{M,i}]^T with entries E_{j,i} from directed transfers.","unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Es","symbol":"E_i^{(s)}","meaning":"M-dimensional vector collecting the energy quantities sold by microgrid i to the other microgrids.","kind":"operational","domain":"Derived vector E_i^{(s)} = [E_{i,1} ... E_{i,M}]^T with entries E_{i,j} from directed transfers.","unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-M","symbol":"M","meaning":"Number of interconnected microgrids participating in the clearing problem.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"M"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-Ec","symbol":"E_i^{(c)}","meaning":"Local energy consumption or demand of microgrid i during the scheduling interval.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"E_i^{(c)}"},"unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g39-e039"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-A","symbol":"A","meaning":"Adjacency matrix A = [a_{i,j}] of the directed microgrid connection topology, with a_{i,j}=1 if a connection from microgrid i to j exists and 0 otherwise; a_{i,i}=0 by convention.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"A = [a_{i,j}]_{M×M}"},"unit":null,"indices":["i=1,...,M","j=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-ei","symbol":"e_i","meaning":"The i-th column of the M×M identity matrix, used to select the i-th component of vector expressions.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"e_i"},"unit":null,"indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g10-e010"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-Ci","symbol":"C_i(·)","meaning":"Generation cost function of microgrid i.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"C_i(·)"},"unit":null,"indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g11-e011"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-gamma","symbol":"γ(·)","meaning":"Transfer cost function common to all directed links.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"γ(·)"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g11-e011"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Computation and disclosure of a microgrid's purchase bid to potential sellers precede the inter-microgrid market-clearing process. Scope: Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e033"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local computation of the energy quantity offered for sale precedes the distributed price-adjustment phase identified as inter-microgrid market clearing. Scope: Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply","clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g24-e024"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Exchanged energy is allocated to minimize global operating cost while satisfying the microgrids' local demands. Scope: System-level allocation of energy exchanged among interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: All microgrids agree to cooperate to minimize total system cost.","expression":"C^* = min_{ {ε_i^{(s)}},{E_{i,j}} } ∑_{i=1}^M C_i(E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)}) + ∑_{i=1}^M e_i^T A^T γ(E_i^{(b)})","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-x-eps","v141-c-R003-x-Eij","v141-c-R003-x-Eb"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-theta-M","v141-c-R003-theta-Ec","v141-c-R003-theta-A","v141-c-R003-theta-ei","v141-c-R003-theta-Ci","v141-c-R003-theta-gamma"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e12","v141-b-g08-e008","v141-b-g15-e015"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Each microgrid's generation plus imports must equal consumption plus exports. Local subproblems constrain offered sales, purchases and residual generation to be nonnegative. Scope: Feasible inter-microgrid allocations and the local optimization subproblems used to compute them.","expression":"E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1); E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0 ∀i; ε_i^{(s)} ≥ 0; E_{j,i} ≥ 0 ∀j","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-x-Eg","v141-c-R003-x-Eb","v141-c-R003-x-Es","v141-c-R003-x-eps","v141-c-R003-x-Eij"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-theta-Ec","v141-c-R003-theta-A","v141-c-R003-theta-ei"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g16-e016","v141-b-g05-e005"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The aggregate energy sold by each microgrid must equal the total energy bought from it by the other microgrids; this coupling constraint governs feasible allocations rather than requiring agreement at every intermediate iteration. Scope: Feasible solutions of the reformulated inter-microgrid allocation problem (4).","expression":"ε_i^{(s)} = e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} ∀i","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-x-eps","v141-c-R003-x-Es","v141-c-R003-x-Eij"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-theta-A","v141-c-R003-theta-ei"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g15-e015","v141-b-g16-e016"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C006","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The allocated energy transfer E_{i,j} must be zero. Scope: Directed inter-microgrid transfer allocations. Condition: There is no directed connection from μG-i to μG-j, so a_{i,j}=0.","expression":"a_{i,j}=0 ⇒ E_{i,j}=0 MWh ∀i,j=1,...,M","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-x-Eij"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-theta-A"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"All generation and transfer cost functions must be positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex and twice differentiable. Scope: Applicability of the proposed distributed inter-microgrid cost-minimization method.","expression":"Each C_i(·) and γ(·) are positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex, and twice differentiable.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-theta-Ci","v141-c-R003-theta-gamma"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g11-e011"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C008","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Nominal generation and link-transfer bounds may be represented by steeply rising cost functions. A microgrid may exceed its nominal generation maximum at significant additional cost. Scope: Optional representation of nominal generation and transfer-capacity bounds in the inter-microgrid allocation problem. Condition: Nominal upper bounds are introduced through cost-function soft constraints.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g12-e012"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e033","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Conclusion","anchor_text":"G 1 keeps local cost functions and local consumption private. More speciﬁcally, each algorithm itera","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"More specifically, each algorithm iteration consists of a local mini-mization step followed by a market clearing process. During the first step, each microgrid computes its local energy bid and reveals it to its potential sellers."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e003","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"propose an algorithm based on dual decomposition that itera- tively solves the problem in a distribu","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Interestingly, each iteration of the resulting algorithm has a straightforward interpretation in economical terms once the new operational variables we introduce are given the meaning of energy prices. First, each microgrid locally computes the amounts of energy it must produce, buy, and sell to minimize its local cost according to the current energy prices. Then, after exchanging the microgrid bids, a regulation phase follows, in which, in a distributed way, the energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand. This two-step process iterates until a global agreement is reached about prices and transferred energies."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e12","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Numerical Results","anchor_text":"local cost substantially. ost In Fig. 1(c), we depict another situation where the topology old heavi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"In this paper, we have addressed a problem in which several microgrids interact by exchanging energy in order to minimize the global operation cost while still satisfying their local demands."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-e004","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON INDUSTRIAL ELECTRONICS, VOL. 62, NO. 4, APRIL 2015 we from an economical point ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Consider a system composed of M interconnected microgrids (μGs) operating in islanded mode. During each scheduling interval, each microgrid μG-i generates E_i^{(g)} units of energy and consumes E_i^{(c)} units of energy. Moreover, μG-i may be allowed to sell energy E_{i,j} to μG-j, j≠i, and to buy energy E_{k,i} from μG-k, k≠i. Then, the energy equilibrium within the μG requires E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g06-e006","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"to sell energy Ei,j to μG j, j̸ i, and to buy energy Ek,i fromrgy μG-k, k̸=i. Then, the energy equil","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where the two M-dimensional column vectors, i.e., E_i^{(b)} = [E_{1,i} ... E_{M,i}]^T, E_i^{(s)} = [E_{i,1} ... E_{i,M}]^T (2) gather all the energy quantities bought and sold, respectively, by μG-i. In addition, we have introduced adjacency matrix A = [a_{i,j}]_{M×M}, where element a_{i,j} is equal to 1 if there exists a connection from μG-i to μG-j and 0 otherwise. Note that, generally, A may be nonsymmetric, meaning that at least two μGs are allowed to exchange energy in one direction only. By convention, we fix a_{i,i}=0. Moreover, a_{i,j}=0 ⇒ E_{i,j}=0 MWh for all i,j=1,...,M."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g08-e008","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"ra- Finally, we further assume that all μGs agree to cooperate gly, with one another in order to min","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Finally, we further assume that all μGs agree to cooperate with one another in order to minimize the total cost of the system. In other words, the energy quantities exchanged by the interconnected μGs form the equilibrium point of the following minimization problem:"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g11-e011","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II-A. Cost Functions","anchor_text":"where achieving the global objective implies a cost reduction at every μG. A. Cost Functions As afor","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"As aforementioned, the algorithm proposed hereafter works with any set of generation/transfer cost functions as long as they all satisfy some mild convexity constraints. Specifically, it is required that {C_i(·)} and γ(·) are positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex, and twice differentiable. Although these requirements may seem abstract and distant from real systems, one should take into account that the cost function of a common electrical generator (e.g., oil, coal, nuclear,...) is often modeled as a quadratic polynomial C(x)=a+bx+cx^2, where coefficients a, b, and c depend on the generator type (see [30] and particularly [31]). Such a generation model clearly satisfies our assumptions, and without available counterexamples, we extended it to the transfer cost function γ(·)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g12-e012","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II-A. Cost Functions","anchor_text":"our assumptions, and without available counterexamples, we q extended it to the transfer cost functi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"It is worth commenting here that the assumptions on the cost functions also allow for a simple way to introduce upper bounds on the energy generated by the μGs or supported by the transfer connections. Indeed, one can introduce soft constraints by designing the cost function with a steep rise at the nominal maximum value (see also the first paragraph in Section IV, where we comment on the cost functions used for simulation). By doing so, the maximum generated/transferred energy quantities are directly controlled by the cost functions without the need for hard constraints (i.e., well-specified inequalities such as E_i^{(g)} ≤ E_{i,max}^{(g)}) that would increase the complexity of the minimization problem in (3). Note that this expedient translates, somehow, to a more flexible system, i.e., when needed, a μG can produce more energy than the nominal maximum if it is willing to pay an (significant) extra cost. Indeed, this situation arises in practical systems when backup generators are activated."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g15-e015","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"i i i i i at {ε(s)i },{Ei,j} i=1 + eTi AT γ(E(b)i ) i=1 s.t. Ei,j ≥0 ∀i,j ks ey E(c)i + ε(s)i −eTi A","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"C^* = min_{ {ε_i^{(s)}},{E_{i,j}} } ∑_{i=1}^M C_i(E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)}) + ∑_{i=1}^M e_i^T A^T γ(E_i^{(b)}) s.t. E_{i,j} ≥ 0 ∀i,j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0 ∀i, ε_i^{(s)} = e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} ∀i. (4) The idea is that, for each μG, we first use the new variable ε_i^{(s)} to represent the energy sold by μG-i and only later force it to be equal to all the energy bought by other μGs from μG-i, i.e., ε_i^{(s)}=e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, which is the coupling constraint."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g16-e016","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"we q y, ( )], y g the M coupling constraints and solving the following dual he problem: er C∗= max C","equation_id":"(5)-(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"C^* = max_λ C(λ) (5) where C(λ) = ∑_{i=1}^M C_i^{(l)}(λ) with the terms C_i^{(l)}(λ) = min_{ε_i^{(s)},E_i^{(b)}} C_i(ε_i^{(s)},E_i^{(b)},λ) s.t. ε_i^{(s)} ≥ 0, E_{j,i} ≥ 0 ∀j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0. (6)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g22-e022","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"μG-i initialize λi[0] repeat μGs exchange {λi[k]} sh μG-i computes ε(s)i [k] and E(b)i [k] by solvin","equation_id":"Algorithm 1","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Algorithm 1 Distributed approach μG-i initialize λ_i[0] repeat μGs exchange {λ_i[k]} μG-i computes ε_i^{(s)}[k] and E_i^{(b)}[k] by solving (6) with fixed λ[k] μG-i informs μG-j, j≠i, about the energy it is willing to buy, namely E_{j,i}[k], at the given price λ_j[k] with energy requests E_{i,j}[k] from neighboring μGs, μG-i builds E_i^{(s)}[k] ← [E_{i,1}[k] ... E_{i,M}[k]]^T μG-i computes λ_i[k+1] ← λ_i[k] + α[k](e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}[k] - ε_i^{(s)}[k]) k ← k+1 until convergence condition is verified"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g24-e024","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":", g ining interpretation: Each Lagrange multiplier λi may be under- costood as the price per energy ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"According to this view, updating step (8) is clearing the market, i.e., prices should be modified until, globally, the energy demand matches the energy offer. Note that (8) is an example of the law of demand: If the energy offered by μG-i ε_i^{(s)}[k] is less than all the energy demanded by the neighboring μGs from μG-i, i.e., e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, then the selling price must increase, and λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e005","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON INDUSTRIAL ELECTRONICS, VOL. 62, NO. 4, APRIL 2015 we from an economical point ","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g10-e010","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"GREGORATTI AND MATAMOROS: DISTRIBUTED ENERGY TRADING: MULTIPLE-MIC where ei is the ith column of the","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where e_i is the ith column of the M×M identity matrix and, with some abuse of notation, we wrote γ(E_i^{(b)})=[γ(E_{1,i}) ... γ(E_{M,i})]^T. In addition, we used (1) to get rid of variables {E_i^{(g)}}. Note that problem (3) considers the μGs as parts of a common system (for instance, they are controlled by the same operator), and the aim is at minimizing the global cost, without focusing on the benefits/losses of each individual μG. We will see later on, however, that the proposed distributed and iterative minimization algorithm opens to a wider sense interpretation, where achieving the global objective implies a cost reduction at every μG."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g39-e039","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"ra- Finally, we further assume that all μGs agree to cooperate gly, with one another in order to min","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For simplicity, we assume that all energy values correspond to constant power generation/absorption/transfer over the scheduling interval."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation","v141-a-M01-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"clearing.inter_area","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e033","quote":"each algorithm iteration consists of a local mini-mization step followed by a market clearing process. During the first step, each microgrid computes its local energy bid and reveals it to its potential sellers."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e003","quote":"First, each microgrid locally computes the amounts of energy it must produce, buy, and sell to minimize its local cost according to the current energy prices. Then, after exchanging the microgrid bids, a regulation phase follows, in which, in a distributed way, the energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g22-e022","quote":"μG-i computes ε_i^{(s)}[k] and E_i^{(b)}[k] by solving (6) with fixed λ[k]"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g24-e024","quote":"According to this view, updating step (8) is clearing the market, i.e., prices should be modified until, globally, the energy demand matches the energy offer."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g08-e008","quote":"Finally, we further assume that all μGs agree to cooperate with one another in order to minimize the total cost of the system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e12","quote":"In this paper, we have addressed a problem in which several microgrids interact by exchanging energy in order to minimize the global operation cost while still satisfying their local demands."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-e004","quote":"Then, the energy equilibrium within the μG requires E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g16-e016","quote":"s.t. ε_i^{(s)} ≥ 0, E_{j,i} ≥ 0 ∀j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0. (6)"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g15-e015","quote":"The idea is that, for each μG, we first use the new variable ε_i^{(s)} to represent the energy sold by μG-i and only later force it to be equal to all the energy bought by other μGs from μG-i, i.e., ε_i^{(s)}=e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, which is the coupling constraint."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g06-e006","quote":"element a_{i,j} is equal to 1 if there exists a connection from μG-i to μG-j and 0 otherwise."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g06-e006","quote":"Moreover, a_{i,j}=0 ⇒ E_{i,j}=0 MWh for all i,j=1,...,M."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g11-e011","quote":"the algorithm proposed hereafter works with any set of generation/transfer cost functions as long as they all satisfy some mild convexity constraints. Specifically, it is required that {C_i(·)} and γ(·) are positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex, and twice differentiable."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g12-e012","quote":"the assumptions on the cost functions also allow for a simple way to introduce upper bounds on the energy generated by the μGs or supported by the transfer connections. Indeed, one can introduce soft constraints by designing the cost function with a steep rise at the nominal maximum value"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g12-e012","quote":"when needed, a μG can produce more energy than the nominal maximum if it is willing to pay an (significant) extra cost."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"24901641dc50e361c1b94bf49c0c90b2f147b9279f528356311da3e8b7176a5c","applicability":"formalized","reason":"All eight C.1 constraints are preserved. C003-C007 are given source-grounded expressions from equations (1), (4), (6) and the stated cost-function condition; C001, C002 and C008 remain procedural or not formalized. The frozen graph has no typed variable or parameter nodes, so x and theta objects are bound only to clearing.inter_area; no nodes, edges, mechanism, stage or constraint meaning are changed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Eij","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The directed transfer E_{i,j} is an operational allocation quantity of the clearing function; the frozen graph has no variable node, so it is attached to clearing.inter_area without adding graph structure."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-x-eps","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The offered-sales variable ε_i^{(s)} is part of the reformulated clearing problem (4) and its local subproblems, represented by clearing.inter_area."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Eg","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Generation E_i^{(g)} enters the clearing feasibility balance (1); it is bound to the clearing node because no dedicated generation node exists in this frozen rule graph."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Eb","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The purchase vector E_i^{(b)} collects directed transfers relevant to the clearing allocation and is attached to clearing.inter_area."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Es","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The sales vector E_i^{(s)} collects directed transfers used by the clearing coupling constraint and is attached to clearing.inter_area."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-M","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"M is the number of microgrids in the clearing problem; it is a fixed system-size input for clearing.inter_area."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-Ec","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_i^{(c)} is the fixed local demand parameter in the clearing balance and objective."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-A","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001","v141-c-R003-E002"],"rationale":"A encodes the directed connection topology that determines admissible transfers in clearing; the two existing precedes edges are retained as context but are not changed."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-ei","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"e_i is the fixed identity-column selector used in the clearing balances and coupling constraint."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-Ci","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C_i(·) is a fixed generation-cost input to the clearing objective and cost-function condition."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-gamma","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"γ(·) is the fixed common transfer-cost input to the clearing objective and cost-function condition."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The global cost-minimization objective defines the allocation performed by clearing.inter_area; no frozen edge represents this objective."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C004","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Energy-balance and local nonnegativity constraints determine feasible clearing allocations at clearing.inter_area."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The coupling constraint is part of the clearing feasibility set and is attached to clearing.inter_area."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C006","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The directed-link zero-transfer condition restricts clearing allocations and is attached to clearing.inter_area."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C007","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The cost-function regularity condition applies to the clearing cost-minimization method represented by clearing.inter_area."}],"revision_requests":["Request B re-extraction verification of equation (3) as printed: the first C_i argument shows - A^T E_i^{(b)} without the e_i^T projector used in equation (4); this patch uses the equation (4) objective form.","Request source support for an explicit functional form of the optional soft upper-bound representation in C008; the supplied evidence states steep cost penalties but gives no general expression, so C008 remains not_formalized.","Request explicit domains and units for E_i^{(g)} and ε_i^{(s)} in the global reformulation: ε_i^{(s)} is constrained nonnegative only in local subproblem (6), and no explicit sign bound on E_i^{(g)} is stated in the supplied evidence for balance (1).","Request node-level variable identities only if downstream validation needs E_{i,j}, ε_i^{(s)}, E_i^{(g)}, E_i^{(b)} and E_i^{(s)} as graph nodes; this patch binds them to clearing.inter_area and does not add or alter graph structure."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
